@meta
id: 27-15
title: Make it stop
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: autocmd
par: 75
minutes: 7
vscode: disabling one extension without disabling the rest
open: standup.md
vimrc: source config.vim

@brief
Add one bullet to the end of `standup.md`:

    - chased finance about the September export

Save it and every bullet in the file turns into a `*`. Somebody's
`config.vim` has an autocommand that rewrites markdown bullets on write,
and it has been doing it to this repo for a week.

You are not fixing `config.vim` today — it is not yours to change. Stop
the autocommand firing in this session, then make the edit and save with
the bullets intact.

@keys
| `:autocmd BufWritePre` | list every autocommand on that event, with its group |
| `:autocmd! dojo` | delete every autocommand in the group `dojo` |
| `:autocmd! BufWritePre *.md` | delete by event and pattern instead |
| `:verbose autocmd BufWritePre` | list them with the file that defined each |
| `:noautocmd w` | one write with all autocommands suppressed |

@hints
Find out what is registered before you try to remove it. `:autocmd
BufWritePre` prints the list, and the name above the entries is its group.
--
`:autocmd!` with a group name after it clears that group. The group here
is called `dojo`.
--
`:autocmd! dojo` `Enter`, then `G`, `o`, type the bullet, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    :autocmd! dojo<Enter>
    Go- chased finance about the September export<Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Sixty-five keystrokes.

The listing you get from `:autocmd BufWritePre` looks like this:

    --- Autocommands ---
    dojo  BufWritePre
        *.md      %s/^- /* /e

The first word is the **group**. Every autocommand belongs to one, and the
group is the handle you use to manage them. `config.vim` looks like this:

    augroup dojo
      autocmd!
      autocmd BufWritePre *.md %s/^- /* /e
    augroup END

Three lines, and the middle one is the one people leave out. `:autocmd`
*adds* — it never replaces. Source a config file twice and you have two
identical autocommands, both firing, and a transformation that is not
idempotent now runs twice. Everyone hits this the first time they
`:source ~/.vimrc` to try a change: the reformat-on-save that worked
yesterday now doubles its work, and nothing in the file looks wrong.

`autocmd!` with no arguments, inside an `augroup`, clears that group
before the block refills it. It makes the block re-runnable. Write every
augroup this way, always, including the ones with a single autocommand in
them:

    augroup myformatting
      autocmd!
      autocmd BufWritePre *.ts %s/\s\+$//e
    augroup END

Outside a group, `:autocmd!` with an event and a pattern deletes matching
autocommands wherever they came from — a blunter instrument, and the one
to reach for when the offender has no group of its own. Everything defined
without an `augroup` lands in a nameless group together, which is precisely
why it is hard to remove one of them and not the rest.

`:noautocmd w` is the escape hatch for a single write. Useful when you
want the autocommand in general and not on this file, this once.

@start config.vim
" team defaults, inherited from the old tech lead
augroup dojo
  autocmd!
  autocmd BufWritePre *.md %s/^- /* /e
augroup END

@start standup.md
# Standup 17 September

- shipped the refund window fix
- reviewed the gateway cache PR

@expected standup.md
# Standup 17 September

- shipped the refund window fix
- reviewed the gateway cache PR
- chased finance about the September export
